WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.435
Domain 1 primer: Agentic Architecture & Orchestration.

2
00:00:04.435 --> 00:00:14.708
Domain 1 is the largest slice of the exam, about 27 percent, and it covers how Claude-based agents loop, delegate, follow rules and remember.

3
00:00:16.108 --> 00:00:20.064
Welcome to domain one, agentic architecture and orchestration.

4
00:00:20.064 --> 00:00:24.594
It is the biggest domain on the exam, about a quarter of the questions.

5
00:00:24.594 --> 00:00:30.529
It is all about systems where Claude takes actions with tools instead of just answering once.

6
00:00:30.529 --> 00:00:37.356
You will meet it mostly in the customer support, multi-agent research and developer productivity scenarios.

7
00:00:38.756 --> 00:00:40.470
A few words to learn first.

8
00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:43.836
A tool is a function Claude can ask your code to run.

9
00:00:43.836 --> 00:00:50.757
The agentic loop is calling Claude, running the tools it asks for, and sending results back until it is done.

10
00:00:50.757 --> 00:00:55.266
A coordinator is a manager agent and subagents are its focused helpers.

11
00:00:55.266 --> 00:00:58.123
Hooks are your own code at fixed checkpoints.

12
00:00:58.123 --> 00:01:00.409
A session is the saved conversation.

13
00:01:01.809 --> 00:01:04.290
Picture an agent system as five parts.

14
00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:07.358
The engine is the loop, steered by stop reason.

15
00:01:07.358 --> 00:01:12.711
The team is a coordinator handing work to subagents that start with a clean slate.

16
00:01:12.711 --> 00:01:16.693
The guardrails are hooks and code gates that hold every time.

17
00:01:16.693 --> 00:01:18.129
Memory is the session.

18
00:01:18.129 --> 00:01:21.393
And the plan is how you split big work into steps.

19
00:01:22.793 --> 00:01:25.734
The seven task statements build on each other.

20
00:01:25.734 --> 00:01:27.844
One point one is the loop itself.

21
00:01:27.844 --> 00:01:32.831
One point two and one point three cover teams of agents and how to brief them.

22
00:01:32.831 --> 00:01:39.224
One point four and one point five are about rules that must always hold, using code gates and hooks.

23
00:01:39.224 --> 00:01:43.316
One point six is decomposition, and one point seven is sessions.

24
00:01:44.716 --> 00:01:47.021
Here are the words this domain uses most.

25
00:01:47.021 --> 00:01:50.113
Pause the video and read through them, then keep going.

26
00:01:51.513 --> 00:01:54.124
Four ideas run through this whole domain.

27
00:01:54.124 --> 00:01:58.580
Trust structured signals, like stop reason, over the words in a reply.

28
00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:01.063
Subagents only know what you hand them.

29
00:02:01.063 --> 00:02:05.265
Anything that must always happen belongs in code, not in a prompt.

30
00:02:05.265 --> 00:02:08.640
And pick the simplest design that solves the problem.

31
00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:11.887
Keep these in mind as you work through the lessons.

